      "case_id": "synthetic/diabetes-education",
      "source": "local-synthetic",
      "hypothesis_transcript": null,
      "hypothesis_note": {
        "subjective": "Susan Callaghan, born 2 November 1968 (57 years old), presents to discuss recent blood test results that indicated a diagnosis of diabetes. She reports that she was informed of the diagnosis by a phone call and expresses uncertainty about its meaning. She asks whether her recent episodes of oral thrush are related, and the clinician confirms that elevated blood sugar can predispose to such infections. She voices concern about long‑term complications, specifically mentioning a coworker with diabetes who has experienced eye and foot problems, and she worries that similar issues could affect her. She acknowledges the need to keep blood sugar controlled and notes that regular eye and foot examinations are part of her care plan. Medication history, allergy status, past medical, surgical, social and family histories were not elicited in this recording. The consultation was conducted by Priya, junior doctor here.",
        "objective": "This recording captures history‑taking only; objective findings such as vital signs, physical examination results, or laboratory values are pending clinical assessment. This recording captures history-taking only; objective findings are pending clinical examination and investigation. Results not documented in the transcript include blood test; results.",
        "assessment": "The patient has a new diagnosis of type 2 diabetes mellitus and a historical episode of oral thrush. She expresses concern about future eye and foot complications related to diabetes, and these risks were discussed with her, emphasizing the need for ongoing screening.",
        "plan": "The clinician provided education on type 2 diabetes pathophysiology, the link between hyperglycaemia and infections such as oral thrush, and the importance of regular eye and foot examinations. Written leaflets were given for reference. The patient was instructed to arrange a follow‑up appointment with her primary‑care physician within the next 2–4 weeks to review the abnormal blood test results, obtain fasting glucose and HbA1c values, discuss lifestyle modifications (diet, exercise) and consider initiating pharmacologic therapy if needed. Referral for retinal screening and a comprehensive foot exam was advised. Safety‑netting instructions included contacting the clinic or seeking urgent care if she experiences worsening symptoms such as increased thirst, polyuria, visual changes, foot ulcers, or recurrent infections."
      },
