    {
      "case_id": "synthetic/medication-review",
      "source": "local-synthetic",
      "hypothesis_transcript": null,
      "hypothesis_note": {
        "subjective": "Michael Osei, DOB 30 June 1957, age 69, presents for a medication review. He reports taking amlodipine each morning and atorvastatin at night but admits to forgetting the evening dose about twice a week. Over the past month his ankles have become puffy. He denies any dizziness or headaches. He has not had his blood pressure checked since last year. Allergies, past medical history, surgical history, social history, and family history were not elicited in this recording.",
        "objective": "This encounter captured history‑taking only; objective findings are pending clinical examination and investigations. No vital signs were documented in the transcript. Blood pressure measurement was planned for today but not yet performed, and laboratory tests for renal function and lipid profile were ordered but results are not available.",
        "assessment": "1. Ankle edema likely secondary to amlodipine therapy, based on recent onset of puffy ankles and the known side‑effect profile of calcium‑channel blockers. 2. Potential uncontrolled hypertension, given the lack of a blood pressure reading for over a year and ongoing amlodipine use. 3. Suboptimal adherence to atorvastatin, with missed doses approximately twice weekly, which may affect lipid control.",
        "plan": "Obtain a blood pressure reading today and review the result; if elevated, consider adjusting antihypertensive therapy or switching amlodipine to an alternative agent. Order renal function panel and lipid profile now and repeat in six weeks to assess kidney status and cholesterol control. Counsel the patient on the likely link between amlodipine and ankle swelling and discuss the option of dose reduction or switching to a different class of antihypertensive if edema persists. Reinforce the importance of consistent atorvastatin intake and provide strategies to improve adherence, such as setting a daily reminder. Schedule a follow‑up appointment in six weeks to reassess ankle swelling, blood pressure, and laboratory results, and to review medication adherence."
      },
